# Analytic single-scattering slab — the ONE mode-J gate that does not trust another
# ftrace mode.
#
# Gates 1/2/4 are all self-referential in the same way: gate 1 says mode `J` with no beams
# equals mode `D`, gate 2 says the incremental MIS weight equals an absolute-form one written
# from the same derivation, and gate 4 says mode `J` equals a converged mode `D`. All three
# would pass unchanged if mode `D` *and* mode `J` shared a common-mode error — a sigma_s
# convention, a phase-function normalisation, a point-light intensity of Phi/(2pi) instead of
# Phi/(4pi). This scene is built so the answer can be computed in closed form (a 1-D
# quadrature with no Monte Carlo anywhere) by `tools/slab_ss_ref.py`, so mode `J` is checked
# against physics rather than against its own siblings.
#
# The construction, and why each piece is what it is:
#
#   * NO GEOMETRY except the emitter. With `-max-bounce 1` the only path that exists is
#     eye -> (one medium scatter) -> light. Nothing can reflect, so the closed form is the
#     whole image, not a term in it.
#   * The emitter is a SPHERE, and a uniformly-emitting Lambertian sphere is EXACTLY a point
#     source of intensity Phi/(4pi) at every external point: its radiant intensity is
#     L_e * pi R^2 in every direction, independent of distance. The medium does not spoil
#     that. Averaging exp(-sigma_t * dist) over the emitting disc gives
#     exp(-sigma_t d) * (2/(sigma_t R)^2) * ((sigma_t R - 1) exp(sigma_t R) + 1), i.e. a
#     multiplier that does NOT depend on d — so it lands in the one global constant the gate
#     already fits, and the reference needs no small-sphere correction. (That derivation
#     assumes the rays from the emitting disc to the receiver are parallel, which costs
#     O((R/d)^2); every scatter point in this frame is at least 1.1 m from the emitter against
#     R = 0.02 m, so that is ~3e-4.)
#   * The emitter is INSIDE the medium (mode `J`'s beams are deposited by light subpaths, and
#     a subpath launched from outside a bounded medium into empty space deposits nothing —
#     mode `J` warns "the beam map is empty" and degenerates to mode `D`, which would make
#     this gate test the wrong half of the estimator). It is nonetheless OUTSIDE the camera
#     frustum — 29.1 deg off the view axis against a 15 deg half-fov — so no camera ray passes
#     near it, the 1/r^2 integrand is never near-singular, the beam x ray estimator's
#     1/sin(theta) tail is never excited, and its own surface is never directly visible: every
#     pixel in the frame is pure single scattering with nothing to mask out.
#   * The medium is BOUNDED (and the bounds are stretched along +x to enclose the emitter), so
#     both the camera-side and light-side transmittances are finite slab integrals the
#     reference clips exactly, and `sigma_t 2` is an optical depth of 4 across the viewed part
#     of the box — thick enough to be a real test of the exponentials, thin enough that the
#     dimmest corner can still be converged.
#   * `g 0` makes the phase function exactly 1/(4pi), so the reference has no phase-function
#     convention to get wrong. (Testing the Henyey-Greenstein convention is a separate gate;
#     mixing it in here would confound a phase error with an estimator error.)
#   * `albedo 1.0`: with a single scattering event albedo is a pure scale on sigma_s, so this
#     only maximises signal.
#
# The reference is known up to ONE global scale constant per channel — the emitter's spectral
# power distribution times ftrace's spectral-to-XYZ-to-linear-sRGB conversion, which is a
# constant of the scene and not of the pixel. So the gate has two independent halves:
#   (a) SHAPE: image / reference must be flat across the frame. This tests every distance- and
#       angle-dependent factor in the estimator — both transmittances, the 1/r^2, the kernel
#       normalisation's radius dependence, the 1/sin(theta) — and no free parameter can absorb
#       an error in any of them.
#   (b) SCALE: the one fitted constant must be the SAME for modes R, D and J. Mode R is a
#       backward path tracer that shares none of the bidirectional or beam machinery, so
#       agreement across all three pins the absolute level.
#
#   ftrace -in scenes/_slab_ss.ftsl -mode R -device cpu -max-bounce 1 -spp 4096 -hdr -o png/slab_r.png
#   ftrace -in scenes/_slab_ss.ftsl -mode D -device cpu -max-bounce 1 -time 600 -hdr -o png/slab_d.png
#   ftrace -in scenes/_slab_ss.ftsl -mode J -device cpu -max-bounce 1 -time 600 -hdr -o png/slab_j.png
#   python tools/slab_ss_ref.py png/slab_r.pfm png/slab_d.pfm png/slab_j.pfm

scene {
    units    meters
    spectral 360 830 1
}

# A pure-scattering isotropic slab. The viewed part is the 2 m cube around the origin; the
# box is stretched to x = 3 only so that it encloses the emitter (see the header).
medium {
    sigma_t 2.0   albedo 1.0   g 0.0
    bounds { min -1 -1 -1   max 3 1 1 }
}

# The only object in the scene. Inside the medium, outside the frame.
light sphere { center 2.5 0 0.5   radius 0.02   spd preset:bb6500 }

camera "cam" {
    eye 0 0 5   look_at 0 0 0   up 0 1 0   fov_y 30
    mode R
    film { res 96 96 }
}
